# bot.ts Modules Rules

A module is a self-contained feature folder under `src/modules/<name>/` that can contain any combination of bot.ts elements. Only modules enabled in `modules.json` (project root) are loaded at boot time.

## Module registry

`modules.json` at the project root controls which modules are active:

```json
{
  "keepDependencies": [],
  "modules": {
    "example": true,
    "ai-assistant": true
  }
}
```

- `modules` — only entries set to `true` are loaded. New directories are auto-added as enabled.
- `keepDependencies` — deps that already existed in `package.json` before a module added them (never removed by the CLI).

Use `bot module enable/disable/install/remove` to manage.

## Module structure

```
src/modules/<name>/
├── module.json       # Dependencies and metadata
├── README.md         # Documentation
├── *.ts              # Root files — exports auto-dispatched by element type
├── commands/         # Textual commands (same as src/commands/)
├── slash/            # Slash commands (same as src/slash/)
├── buttons/          # Button handlers (same as src/buttons/)
├── listeners/        # Event listeners (same as src/listeners/)
├── cron/             # Cron jobs (same as src/cron/)
├── tables/           # Database tables (same as src/tables/)
└── namespaces/       # Shared utilities (imported via #modules/<name>/namespaces/<file>)
```

Every subdirectory is optional — only include what the module needs.

## Root files

`.ts` files at the module root are imported and all exports with a `type` property are dispatched to the matching handler. This allows grouping multiple elements in one file:

```typescript
// src/modules/my-module/setup.ts
import { SlashCommand } from "#core/slash"
import { Listener } from "#core/listener"

export const greetCmd = new SlashCommand({
  name: "greet",
  description: "Greet someone",
  async run(interaction) { await interaction.reply("Hello!") },
})

export const welcomeListener = new Listener({
  event: "guildMemberAdd",
  description: "Welcome new members",
  async run(member) { await member.guild.systemChannel?.send(`Welcome ${member}!`) },
})
```

Non-element exports are silently ignored.

## module.json

Declare module dependencies (used by `bot module enable/install` to add them to `package.json`):

```json
{
  "name": "my-module",
  "description": "What the module does",
  "dependencies": { "some-package": "^1.0.0" }
}
```

## Element file patterns

Files inside subdirectories follow the **exact same patterns** as their top-level counterparts:

```typescript
// src/modules/leveling/commands/rank.ts
import { Command } from "#core/command"

export default new Command({
  name: "rank",
  description: "Show your level",
  channelType: "guild",
  async run(message) {
    await message.channel.send(`Level for ${message.author}`)
  },
})
```

## Importing module utilities

Use the `#modules/*` path alias:

```typescript
import { greet } from "#modules/leveling/namespaces/helpers"
```

## Key constraints

- Module name must be kebab-case (e.g. `leveling`, `mod-tools`)
- Element names must be globally unique (a module command cannot have the same name as a top-level command)
- Listener files still use `category.event.ts` naming
- `.native.ts` override pattern works the same way inside modules
- Built-in modules: `example` (reference), `ai-assistant` (AI-powered bot management)
